{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793197867,"nonce":"tdHbG2ILD+5kGxqrAnmLpunIGMM=","title":"Native named layer samples are recorded but draw no pixels","message":"A named opaque red layer paints correctly at its source, but sample_layer of that output at another destination leaves the blue background. Reproduced offscreen on 3c901e1635a9d31c56cc8ff6fc61ac666f4b2e2f; /private/tmp/blinc-named-negative.log reports [0,0,255,255] instead of [255,0,0,255] at the sample. The initial fixture used a nonexistent viewport setter; that compile error was corrected before the runtime negative.\n\nThe native renderer ignores LayerCommand::Sample, and the paint recorder always writes samples into the background static batch. This is a pre-existing missing feature, not a newly proven regression from collected groups. It is part of the named-Sample gap in parent e0a7502e3e9d71bb983572df7db0033a67da1c48262e53368ae2399bc22e583a.\n\nScoped fix: ordered pass-local named output retention with source-rectangle sampling, correct destination transform/clip/opacity, active static/motion/composite/foreground routing, nested group/effect ordering, and cleanup after each pass. Names are not a persistent cross-frame or cross-window texture store. Unknown/forward references draw nothing. Independent ordered passes do not share names; arbitrary cross-pass ownership remains in the parent.\n\nVerify copied pixels, clips/transforms, nested opacity/effects, RGBA/path ownership, offscreen/padded sources, duplicate/zero/unknown names, replay and pool reuse; normal and data-texture GPU paths with jobs=2 and scoped adjacent controls only. No push or publication.","files":null}]}